inventory for the qb package: if a component is vendored under qb/, it is listed
here, and the check in scripts/check-vendor-attribution.py fails the build's guard
battery when a vendored unit is missing its attribution.

INSTALLED LOCATION
------------------
When qb is installed (cmake --install), this file and every license text referenced
below are copied to:

    <prefix>/share/licenses/qb/

so a downstream redistributor can discharge their own notice obligations from the
installed tree alone, without going back to the source repository.

Components whose license text is carried inside the installed header itself are
marked "in-header" and have no separate file — the notice travels with the code.


1. nlohmann/json
   -------------
   Upstream    https://github.com/nlohmann/json
   Author      Niels Lohmann <https://nlohmann.me>
   Version     3.12.0 as declared by NLOHMANN_JSON_VERSION_* — see the NOTE below
   License     MIT
   Vendored    modules/nlohmann/json.hpp
   Installed   <prefix>/include/nlohmann/json.hpp
   License at  <prefix>/share/licenses/qb/third-party/nlohmann/LICENSE.MIT

   The single-header amalgamation carries an SPDX-License-Identifier line but not
   the MIT permission notice, so the accompanying LICENSE.MIT is what satisfies
   MIT's requirement that the permission notice ship with the software.

   NOTE — the vendored copy is NOT the released 3.12.0 artifact. It declares
   NLOHMANN_JSON_VERSION_MAJOR/MINOR/PATCH = 3/12/0 but diverges from the v3.12.0
   tag (452 added / 266 removed lines) and carries changes that exist only on
   upstream's develop branch after that tag — among them the
   `#if defined(__cpp_lib_byte)` guard around the std::byte char_traits
   specialization. It is an untagged post-3.12.0 develop snapshot.

   Consequence for consumers: nlohmann/json puts the version into its inline
   namespace (nlohmann::json_abi_v3_12_0). A program that links this copy together
   with a genuine 3.12.0 gets one inline namespace spanning two different sets of
   definitions — an ODR violation the linker will not diagnose. Consumers who
   already own nlohmann/json should build qb against theirs; qb detects a system
   nlohmann_json and then installs neither this header nor its license.

